          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the smallest treadmill that is still usable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walking, a walking pad at 4 to 6 inches tall and 60 to 90 lbs is the smallest thing that works, and it slides under a bed. For a full treadmill with handrails and incline, expect a machine that stands folded against a wall rather than one that disappears, and check the folded height as well as the footprint. Manufacturer figures can quote the deck rather than the widest point.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do folding treadmills actually save space?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you fold them, and that depends almost entirely on the mechanism. A hydraulic assist that takes no effort is the kind that keeps being used; a manual latch needing real effort tends to stop being folded after the first few weeks. Judge a manual-fold machine on its unfolded footprint, because that is what you will live with.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much clearance does a treadmill need in a small room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Leave as much run-off space behind the deck as the room allows — it is where you go if you lose your footing, not a preference. You also need clearance for the swing arc while the deck rotates upright, which is larger than the folded footprint and is the measurement people most often miss.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill go under a bed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A walking pad can — most are 4 to 6 inches tall and slide underneath easily. A conventional folding treadmill cannot: even folded they stand 55 to 60 inches tall and are designed to stand against a wall rather than lie flat. If under-bed storage is the requirement, you are shopping for a walking pad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are compact treadmills worse to run on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes, and the tension is unavoidable. The properties that make a machine easy to store — a light frame and a short deck — are the same ones that make it less composed at pace and louder for anyone below you. If you intend to run and you are over about 5'8", you need a 60-inch deck, which rules out most of the smallest options.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is folded height or folded footprint more important?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the room. Fully upright machines have the smallest floor footprint and stand 55 to 65 inches tall, which suits a wall beside a wardrobe and not an alcove under a shelf. Measure the vertical clearance where the machine will be stored before assuming a small footprint solves the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

