          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two are measurement errors and the third is the reason most folding treadmills stop being folded.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the published folded dimensions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Published figures can quote the deck rather than the widest point including the console and wheels. On a machine being fitted into an alcove, a few inches is the difference between working and being returned. Measure at the widest and deepest points.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the swing arc</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The deck sweeps through an arc as it rotates upright, and it needs clear space above and in front of the pivot while it moves. A machine that cannot fold in its storage position against a shelf has to be pulled forward first — which is exactly the sort of extra step that stops folds happening at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a folding treadmill will get folded</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Fold effort, not folded size, decides this. A hydraulic assist keeps being used because it takes no effort; a manual latch tends to be quietly abandoned after the first few weeks. Nobody makes a decision to stop — it just becomes one more thing. Judge a manual-fold machine on its unfolded footprint.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Positioning for storage rather than for use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill facing a blank wall because that was the tidiest position is noticeably less pleasant to spend forty minutes on, and a machine stored in another room gets used in April and not in January. Put it where you already spend time and where there is something to look at, then solve the storage around that.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
